# Telga release signing — TEMPLATE.
#
# Copy to `keystore.properties` (which .gitignore refuses) and fill in.
# The real file, and the .jks it points at, are secrets in the sense of
# CLAUDE.md §24: never committed, never pasted into a chat or an issue, never
# emailed, never stored in the vault.
#
# Why this matters more than an ordinary credential: Google Play binds the
# app's identity to this key. Lose it and no update can ever be published for
# the listing again — the only remedy is a new listing with a new name and no
# installed base. Leak it and somebody else can sign a package that installs
# over Telga on a merchant's phone.
#
# Keep the .jks outside this repository. Back it up somewhere that is not the
# same machine, and record who holds it in the vault's Founders and Roles note
# — the key is an operational dependency with exactly one point of failure.
#
# Create one with:
#   keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore telga-release.jks -keyalg RSA \
#     -keysize 4096 -validity 10000 -alias telga
#
# `-validity 10000` is about 27 years. Play requires a key valid past 2033;
# a short-lived key strands the listing when it expires.

# Absolute path to the keystore, or a path relative to apps/mobile/android/.
# Pointing this inside the repository would defeat the .gitignore rules above.
storeFile=C:/Users/you/telga-keys/telga-release.jks

storePassword=
keyAlias=telga
keyPassword=
